# PHASE 7 — GITHUB INTEGRATION

GitHub is the intended code-publishing integration.

Do not add Render integration, hosting-provider integrations, deployment wizards, or automatic hosting workflows.

Users will handle deployment independently outside this platform after pushing code to GitHub.

Implement a secure GitHub workflow:

1. Connect a GitHub account using an appropriate OAuth or supported authorization flow.
2. Select an existing repository or create one where permissions allow.
3. Select repository visibility.
4. Choose a branch.
5. Enter a commit message.
6. Review files and changes.
7. Confirm and push.
8. Display progress, success, authentication errors, permission errors, and retry options.

Requirements:

- Use the official supported GitHub API and authentication practices.
- Keep access tokens and refresh tokens on the server or in an appropriate secure credential store.
- Request the minimum permissions required.
- Respect repository visibility and private-repository settings.
- Do not expose access tokens in browser storage, logs, URLs, or client-side bundles.
- Avoid accidental overwrites and provide clear conflict handling.
- Never report a successful push until GitHub confirms it.

If GitHub configuration is unavailable, identify the missing setup and implement the integration only after approval and valid configuration.

